# Yarning with IEEE 1164

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:ieee1164, path: "."},
  {:kino, "~> 0.13"}
])
```

---

## Before we begin

Knowledge is relational. So we'll yarn with IEEE 1164 and see where the journey takes us.

The journey starts in 1993, but the wisdom is much older.

Each chapter arrives one at a time. Run the cells in order. There is no hurry.

```elixir
# Load the story as a list of chapters, each building on the last.
chapters = Diffo.Ieee1164.stream_integrated() |> Enum.to_list()
sections = Diffo.Ieee1164.yarn() |> Keyword.keys()

# A small helper — shows what each chapter knows.
defmodule Chapter do
  def names(artefact) do
    artefact.nodes
    |> Enum.map(fn {_, data} -> data[:properties]["name"] end)
    |> Enum.reject(&is_nil/1)
    |> Enum.sort()
  end

  def new_names(artefact, previous) do
    prev = names(previous) |> MapSet.new()
    names(artefact) |> Enum.reject(&MapSet.member?(prev, &1))
  end

  def summary(artefact, previous \\ nil) do
    total = map_size(artefact.nodes)
    rels = length(artefact.relationships)
    new = if previous, do: new_names(artefact, previous), else: names(artefact)
    IO.puts("  #{total} nodes · #{rels} relationships")
    IO.puts("  arrived: #{Enum.join(new, ", ")}")
  end
end

:ready
```

---

## Chapter 1 — Standard speaks

IEEE 1164 introduces itself. It names the things it defines — not the things themselves, not yet. Just their names, held in relation to the standard that brings them into being.

```elixir
[ch1 | _] = chapters
Chapter.summary(ch1)
```

---

## Chapter 2 — Signals

The signal family speaks. `std_ulogic` is the unresolved truth — what a driver actually drives. `std_logic` is its resolved form, what the wire becomes when all the drivers are heard. `std_logic_vector` scales it.

```elixir
[ch1, ch2 | _] = chapters
Chapter.summary(ch2, ch1)
```

---

## Chapter 3 — Values

Nine voices. Each distinct. Each with something to say.

```elixir
[_, ch2, ch3 | _] = chapters
Chapter.summary(ch3, ch2)
```

> *"I am unknown."*
> *"I am unknowable."*
> *"I am twin brother of 1."*
> *"I am twin brother of 0."*
> *"I am you unchanged."*
> *"I can be you unchanged."*
> *"I am twin sister of H."*
> *"I am twin sister of L."*
> *"I am anyone."*

---

## Chapter 4 — Resolutions

Conflict is not failure. It is an opportunity to resolve. The values meet on the wire and the resolution function decides who is heard.

`forces` speaks loudest. `weakly_forces` speaks loudly but yields. `unknown_overrides_known` spreads uncertainty. `strength_overrides_conflict` brings order.

```elixir
[_, _, ch3, ch4 | _] = chapters
Chapter.summary(ch4, ch3)
```

---

## Chapter 5 — Operations

NOT knows your polarity but not your strength. AND knows zero speaks loudest. OR knows one speaks loudest. XOR knows the difference — same is silence, different is voice.

```elixir
[_, _, _, ch4, ch5 | _] = chapters
Chapter.summary(ch5, ch4)
```

---

## Chapter 6 — Projections

Not everyone needs all nine values. Some worlds are simpler. The projections let you choose your world — and know what you are giving up.

`std_logic_world` holds all nine. `x01_world` keeps certainty and names the rest unknowable. `x01z_world` keeps high impedance. `ux01_world` remembers the uninitialised. `bit_world` keeps only two.

```elixir
[_, _, _, _, ch5, ch6 | _] = chapters
Chapter.summary(ch6, ch5)
```

---

## Chapter 7 — Transitions

The moment of becoming. `departing` is what you were. `arriving` is what you are becoming. `rising_edge` and `falling_edge` read both — they use `to_x01` to see clearly through the noise, then speak only when the transition is real.

```elixir
[_, _, _, _, _, ch6, ch7 | _] = chapters
Chapter.summary(ch7, ch6)
```

---

## Chapter 8 — Synchronicity

This is where diffo joins the yarn.

IEEE 1164 gave us everything else. But synchronicity is the gift that makes it useful — the clock that marks the moment of knowing. The heartbeat that governs what is sampled, what is held, what belongs to a domain.

*With one breath, with one flow, you will know synchronicity.*

```elixir
[_, _, _, _, _, _, ch7, ch8] = chapters
Chapter.summary(ch8, ch7)

IO.puts("\n  The story is complete.")
IO.puts("  #{map_size(ch8.nodes)} nodes · #{length(ch8.relationships)} relationships")
```

---

## Your Glyph

The glyph is the resolution matrix — the whole of what you have just heard, rendered as pattern. It does not explain itself. To read it, you had to make the journey.

Choose the one that names when you came to know IEEE 1164.

```elixir
glyph_dir = Path.join(__DIR__, "images/glyphs")

glyphs = [
  {"Green Phosphor", "known at the terminal",       "std_logic_1164_green_phosphor.svg"},
  {"Hercules",       "known in the home lab",        "std_logic_1164_hercules.svg"},
  {"X11",            "known at the workstation",     "std_logic_1164_x11.svg"},
  {"Dark",           "known now",                   "std_logic_1164_black.svg"}
]

widgets =
  Enum.map(glyphs, fn {title, era, filename} ->
    path = Path.join(glyph_dir, filename)
    svg  = File.read!(path)

    preview  = Kino.Markdown.new("<div style='text-align:center'>#{svg}</div>")
    label    = Kino.Markdown.new("**#{title}**  \n*#{era}*")
    download = Kino.Download.new(
      fn -> {svg, [filename: filename, mime_type: "image/svg+xml"]} end,
      label: "Download"
    )

    Kino.Layout.grid([preview, label, download], columns: 1)
  end)

Kino.Layout.grid(widgets, columns: 4)
```
